insight_0:
- Significantly lowering the product price can lead to an increase in sales (see Rounds 100-120, 150-170);
   - Lower pricing does not always result in higher profits due to low margin, as demonstrated in some instances where high quantity sold resulted in minimal profit (e.g., Rounds 183 and 193). Conversely, maintaining a moderate price can lead to reasonable sales and substantial profit, as observed after Round 200;
   - Price increases or decreases do not seem to have long-lasting effects on consumer behavior and market demand. Changes in pricing should be considered strategically, assessing potential shifts in the competitive landscape and market dynamics (e.g., Rounds 150-170 vs. 180).
